I have to agree on this one, though instead of bwuahaha I'd just say heh heh.

Here's how the difficulty goes (without too many hard conducts):

1) First few levels: Pretty hard, you may have to fight even with your nose hair
to survive. Surviving this and the second part feels like a major accomplishment.
2) Getting AC around 0: Nice, a bit easier, don't level up too quickly now.
3) Getting reflection and MR and/or ac about -2: For spoiled/experienced player things
got much easier. Minotaurs, for example, and large groups at once can kill you, though.
4) Getting /WoW from Castle: From now on, more easy. Amulet run and
especially Astral Plane pose a nice challenge (Pestilence... shudder).
5) The endgame isn't too difficult if experience level is not too high, so
e.g. Archons won't start summoning storms.

So, after a player has survived long enough to get the Armageddon's Armanent
Ascension Kit, well, it's fun to start hacking the monsters instead of getting nearly
killed. I don't mind being (over)powerful after barely surviving the initial levels.
Of course, after getting a near-complete AK, there's the boring part; Gehennom
(mazes). I play through it only to get to the Elemental Planes, which are
definately better than Gehennom.

"Gehennom is boring. Lots of mazes... very unoriginal. Oh, and there could be
some very strong monsters there." (The Gehennom Comments, page 36)

Still,  I wouldn't fight boredom or tediousness by increasing difficulty;
I'd rather try different classes, different servers etc. And there are the conducts...
not that I haven't tried the really hard ones (atheist, pacifist, illiterate, foodless).
I'm not saying that conducts are the ultimate remedy for the tediousness of
endgame mopping, but at least they increase difficulty.

